      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\), we need to ensure that the expression inside the square root is positive. This is because the square root function is only defined for non-negative numbers, and the denominator of a fraction cannot be zero.\n\nHere are the steps to find the domain:\n\n1. **Identify the condition for the square root:**\n   The expression inside the square root, \\( x \\), must be positive:\n   \\[\n   x > 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Express the domain in interval notation:**\n   The inequality \\( x > 0 \\) indicates that \\( x \\) can take any value greater than 0. In interval notation, this is written as:\n   \\[\n   (0, \\infty)\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, \\infty)}\\).",
